# Zustand State Management

## When to Use React Context vs Zustand

### When to Use React Context

**Use React Context for:**

- ✅ **Simple global state** that changes infrequently
  - Theme provider (light/dark mode)
  - Authentication context (user info, login status)
  - Language/locale settings
  - Feature flags that rarely change

- ✅ **Small to medium applications** where performance isn't critical

- ✅ **Zero dependencies** - Built into React, no external libraries

- ✅ **Read-only or mostly read-only state** that doesn't update often

**Characteristics:**

| Aspect             | Details
|:-------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Performance**    | ❌ Can cause unnecessary re-renders - all consumers re-render when context value changes |
| **Setup**          | ✅ Minimal - Built into React, simple Provider pattern                                   |
| **State Updates**  | ❌ All consumers re-render on any change - must split contexts to optimize               |
| **Persistence**    | ❌ Manual implementation required - must use localStorage manually                       |
| **DevTools**       | ❌ Limited debugging - React DevTools only, no time-travel                               |
| **Middleware**     | ❌ No middleware support - must implement manually                                       |
| **TypeScript**     | ⚠️ Manual type definitions - context type inference can be tricky                        |
| **Bundle Size**    | ✅ 0 KB (built-in)                                                                       |
| **Learning Curve** | ✅ Low - Part of React core                                                              |
|:-------------------|:-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|

**Example:**
```typescript
// Simple theme context - perfect for Context
const ThemeContext = createContext<'light' | 'dark'>('light');

// Auth context with user info (rarely changes)
const AuthContext = createContext<User | null>(null);
```

### When to Use Zustand

**Use Zustand for:**

- ✅ **Complex state** with frequent updates
  - UI preferences (sidebar, modals, notifications)
  - Form state that needs to be shared across components
  - Multi-step wizards
  - Shopping cart state

- ✅ **Medium to large applications** where performance matters

- ✅ **State that needs persistence** (localStorage/sessionStorage)

- ✅ **Multiple slices of related state** that benefit from organization

- ✅ **Performance-critical scenarios** requiring selective re-renders

**Characteristics:**

| Aspect             | Details
|:-------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Performance**    | ✅ Optimized re-renders - only components selecting changed state re-render          |
| **Setup**          | ⚠️ Requires external library - minimal boilerplate, slice pattern for organization   |
| **State Updates**  | ✅ Selective re-renders - use selectors to subscribe to specific state               |
| **Persistence**    | ✅ Built-in `persist` middleware - easy localStorage/sessionStorage integration      |
| **DevTools**       | ✅ Redux DevTools integration - time-travel debugging, action tracking               |
| **Middleware**     | ✅ Rich middleware ecosystem - `immer`, `subscribeWithSelector`, etc.                |
| **TypeScript**     | ✅ Excellent TypeScript support - type-safe selectors, infer types from store        |
| **Bundle Size**    | ⚠️ ~1 KB (small library)                                                             |
| **Learning Curve** | ⚠️ Moderate - Additional API to learn                                                |
|:-------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|

**Example:**
```typescript
// Complex UI state with frequent updates - perfect for Zustand
const useUIStore = create((set) => ({
  sidebarOpen: false,
  modals: {},
  notifications: [],
  // ... many state properties
}));

// User preferences (needs persistence)
const usePreferencesStore = create(
  persist((set) => ({ /* ... */ }), { name: 'preferences' })
);
```

### Important: NOT for Server State

- ❌ **Both Context and Zustand should NOT be used for server data**
- ✅ **Use TanStack Query** for API data, caching, and server state management

## Store Architecture

### Slice Pattern

Organize stores using the slice pattern for better maintainability. Each slice is a separate `StateCreator` that can access and modify the full store state:

```typescript
// stores/slices/ui-slice.ts
import type { StateCreator } from "zustand";

interface UISlice {
  sidebarOpen: boolean;
  toggleSidebar: () => void;
}

interface ThemeSlice {
  theme: "light" | "dark";
  setTheme: (theme: "light" | "dark") => void;
}

interface SharedSlice {
  resetUI: () => void;
}

// UI slice can access and modify UI state
const createUISlice: StateCreator<
  UISlice & ThemeSlice,
  [["zustand/immer", never]],
  [],
  UISlice
> = (set) => ({
  sidebarOpen: false,
  toggleSidebar: () =>
    set((state) => {
      state.sidebarOpen = !state.sidebarOpen;
    }),
});

// Theme slice can access and modify theme state
const createThemeSlice: StateCreator<
  UISlice & ThemeSlice,
  [["zustand/immer", never]],
  [],
  ThemeSlice
> = (set) => ({
  theme: "light",
  setTheme: (theme) =>
    set((state) => {
      state.theme = theme;
    }),
});

// Shared slice can access methods from other slices using `get()`
const createSharedSlice: StateCreator<
  UISlice & ThemeSlice,
  [["zustand/immer", never]],
  [],
  SharedSlice
> = (set, get) => ({
  resetUI: () => {
    // You can reuse previous methods
    get().toggleSidebar();
    get().setTheme("light");
    // Or set directly
    set((state) => {
      state.sidebarOpen = false;
      state.theme = "light";
    });
  },
});
```

### Store Composition

Combine slices in the main store using `devtools(immer(...))` as recommended by Zustand:

```typescript
// stores/index.ts
import { create } from "zustand";
import { devtools } from "zustand/middleware";
import { immer } from "zustand/middleware/immer";

export const useUIStore = create<UISlice & ThemeSlice & SharedSlice>()(
  devtools(
    immer((...a) => ({
      ...createUISlice(...a),
      ...createThemeSlice(...a),
      ...createSharedSlice(...a),
    })),
    { name: "UIStore", enabled: import.meta.env.DEV },
  ),
);
```

## Middleware Usage

### Immer Middleware

Use `immer` middleware for cleaner immutable updates:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - With immer
set((state) => {
  state.userPreferences.theme = theme;
});

// ❌ BAD - Without immer (more verbose)
set((state) => ({
  ...state,
  userPreferences: {
    ...state.userPreferences,
    theme: theme,
  },
}));
```

### Persist Middleware

Use `persist` for state that should survive page refreshes:

```typescript
persist(
  (set) => ({ /* store */ }),
  {
    name: 'user-preferences-storage',
    partialize: (state) => ({ userPreferences: state.userPreferences }),
  }
)
```

### Devtools Middleware

Always use `devtools` in development for debugging. Use the `enabled` flag to conditionally enable devtools:

```typescript
devtools(
  (set) => ({ /* store */ }),
  {
    name: 'UserPreferencesStore',
    enabled: import.meta.env.DEV, // Only enable in development
  }
)
```

## Component Usage Patterns

### Selector Optimization

**Always use selectors** to prevent unnecessary re-renders:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Subscribes to entire store, causes re-renders on any change
const { userPreferences, otherState } = useStore();

// ✅ GOOD - Only subscribes to specific slice
const userPreferences = useStore((state) => state.userPreferences);

// ✅ GOOD - Multiple selectors for different slices
const theme = useStore((state) => state.userPreferences.theme);
const language = useStore((state) => state.userPreferences.language);
```

### Action Separation

Separate actions to prevent re-renders when actions change:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Actions don't cause re-renders
const setTheme = useStore((state) => state.setTheme);
const theme = useStore((state) => state.userPreferences.theme);

// Or access actions directly from store (no re-render)
const handleThemeChange = () => {
  useStore.getState().setTheme('dark');
};
```

### Shallow Comparison for Objects

Use `useShallow` when selecting multiple properties:

```typescript
import { useShallow } from "zustand/react/shallow";

// ✅ GOOD - Prevents re-renders when object reference changes but values don't
const { theme, language } = useStore(
  useShallow((state) => ({
    theme: state.userPreferences.theme,
    language: state.userPreferences.language,
  }))
);
```

## Type Safety

### Store Types

Define store types explicitly:

```typescript
// stores/types.ts
export interface Store {
  userPreferences: UserPreferences;
}

export interface UserPreferencesActions {
  setTheme: (theme: Theme) => void;
  setLanguage: (language: Language) => void;
}

export type StoreActions = UserPreferencesActions & {
  resetStore: () => void;
};
```

### StateCreator Types

Use proper `StateCreator` types for slices:

```typescript
import type { StateCreator } from "zustand";

// StateCreator<StoreType, MiddlewareTypes, AdditionalMiddleware, SliceType>
const createUISlice: StateCreator<
  UISlice & ThemeSlice,           // Full store type (what this slice can access)
  [["zustand/immer", never]],    // Middleware types (immer in this case)
  [],                             // Additional middleware (empty array)
  UISlice                         // What this slice returns
> = (set) => ({ /* ... */ });
```

**Type Parameters Explained:**
- **First parameter**: The complete store type (all slices combined) - allows the slice to access other slices' state
- **Second parameter**: Middleware types tuple - `[["zustand/immer", never]]` for immer middleware
- **Third parameter**: Additional middleware (usually empty `[]`)
- **Fourth parameter**: The return type of this specific slice

## Best Practices

### 1. Keep Stores Focused

Each slice should handle a single domain concern:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Single responsibility
createUserPreferencesSlice
createUISlice
createAuthSlice

// ❌ BAD - Mixed concerns
createMixedSlice // Handles both UI and user preferences
```

### 2. Use Default State

Define default state separately for reusability:

```typescript
// stores/default-state.ts
export const defaultUserPreferences: UserPreferences = {
  theme: 'light',
  language: 'en',
  dateFormat: 'YYYY-MM-DD',
};

export const defaultStoreState: Store = {
  userPreferences: defaultUserPreferences,
};
```

### 3. Subscribe to Changes

Use `subscribeWithSelector` for side effects:

```typescript
import { subscribeWithSelector } from "zustand/middleware";

useStore.subscribe(
  (state) => state.userPreferences.theme,
  (theme) => {
    // Side effect when theme changes
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', theme);
  }
);
```

### 4. Reset Store

Provide a reset function for testing and logout scenarios:

```typescript
resetStore() {
  set(defaultStoreState);
}
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't Store Server State

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Server state in Zustand
const useStore = create((set) => ({
  users: [], // Should be in React Query
  fetchUsers: async () => {
    const users = await api.getUsers();
    set({ users });
  },
}));

// ✅ GOOD - Server state in React Query
const useUsers = () => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['users'],
    queryFn: () => userService.getUsers(),
  });
};
```

### ❌ Don't Subscribe to Entire Store

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Causes unnecessary re-renders
const store = useStore();

// ✅ GOOD - Select specific state
const theme = useStore((state) => state.userPreferences.theme);
```

### ❌ Don't Mutate State Directly (Without Immer)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Direct mutation without immer
const increment = () => {
  const state = useStore.getState();
  state.count++; // Mutates state directly
  useStore.setState(state);
};

// ✅ GOOD - With immer middleware
const increment = () => {
  set((state) => {
    state.count++; // Immer handles immutability
  });
};
```
